<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint-Pol-de-Léon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint-Pol-de-Léon is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finistere">Finistère</a>, north-western Brittany, near the Channel coast. It takes its name from Paul Aurelian, a Welsh monk held to have founded the bishopric of Léon there in the 6th century. Its former cathedral and the tall spire of the Kreisker chapel dominate the town, which is now a centre of market gardening.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-pol-de-leon.jpg" alt="Saint-Pol-de-Léon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tourisme Roscoff from France · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint-Pol_de_L%C3%A9on_-_Les_3_tours.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Saint-Pol-de-Léon, in Breton Kastell-Paol, is a commune in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finistere">Finistère</a> department of Brittany, on the coast of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It lies on a stretch of about 13 kilometres of coastline, with beaches and creeks, and is linked to the Sainte-Anne islet by a strand of sediment called the Groue, which forms the bay of Pempoul with its harbour. Its inhabitants are called Saintpolitains. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name of the region is Léon, in French Pays Léonard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A well-preserved double stone alley is the last remnant of prehistoric occupation on the site, and several vestiges show a Roman military presence in the 3rd century. From the 6th century the monastery site became an episcopate, called Kastell Paol, and the city grew beyond its walls. It was long the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and became a sort of religious capital. In the 15th century it was a noted spiritual and cultural centre, the Pempoul harbour was rising, and fortified gates protected its 2,000 inhabitants until the 18th century.</p><p>A battle of the Breton War of Succession was fought there in the 14th century, in which the Montfortists and their English allies defeated an army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-of-blois">Charles of Blois</a>. In the 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Léon strongly influenced the city's architecture: a new episcopal house was built in 1706 and extended in 1750, the seminary dates from 1708 and the Catholic college from 1788.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> took away the episcopate, a rich chapter, a college and many religious houses, and a hundred years of economic decline began; Flaubert judged in 1847 that the town was a dead city. The railway of 1883 expanded agricultural production, and in 1890 the town became the main commercial centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for the export of vegetables.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The former cathedral, of the 13th century, stands on the site of the original founded by Paul Aurelian in the 6th century and shows the influence of Norman art on Breton architecture. The Kreisker chapel, of the 14th and 15th centuries, has the highest steeple in Brittany at 80 metres. The old episcopal palace is now the town hall, with a monumental staircase, and the town keeps a 16th-century prebendal house in Breton Renaissance style and the Kéroulas manor, built for a noble canon around 1520. The region remains a strong centre of horticultural production.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint-Pol-de-Léon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its life centred on monastic foundations such as Saint-Malo, Dol and <b>Saint-Pol-de-Léon</b>, linked to saints from Wales and Cornwall.</blockquote><a href="/state/domnonee">Domnonée</a></li></ul></section>
